portfolio. Our approach involves combining an understanding of core park standards
with expression of the unique qualities found in a specifi c place. We are constantly
inspired by culture, history, science and the possibilities of the future.
Cheyenne off ers many forms of inspiration that can contribute to shaping a park
that is both memorable and signifi cant. As the capital of Wyoming, Cheyenne off ers
a wide range of architectural styles spanning decades through diff erent eras. These
style provide visual interest and rich material options. Examples of neoclassicism, arts
and crafts, Victorian, modern and contemporary styles surround the site. The West
Edge has a strong railroad and warehouse presence that also speaks to Cheyenne’s
heritage. The prominence of brick and stone used with a clear street grid and zero lot
line building massing creates a framework for the West Edge to host new businesses
and residences.
The combination of the classic geometry, cultural history and storm water elements
all off er sources of design themes and concepts to strengthen the quality of the fi nal
park design.
Placemaking
Improving a neighborhood, city or region through Placemaking is a principle that we
fi rmly stand behind. We want to inspire the community to improve and strengthen the
connection between people and places they share. We want to help the community
create inspiring destinations through a collaborative eff ort. Placemaking helps to
improve the sociability, access to the surrounding community, image of the community
and provides additional uses and activities. We design places that are socially diverse,
inclusive and welcoming.
Theming/Site Character
DHM Design believes that theming creates successful, memorable public spaces
that inspire and spark spontaneous play children. Many of our parks are inspired by
nature, art, history and literature. By creating a themed atmosphere of imaginative
play, it provides children with both cognitive and social benefi ts by helping to acquire
knowledge and understanding through thought, experience and use of senses. It
provides children an opportunity to develop their verbal skills, creativity and social
interaction.
Theming helps direct design decisions and provides continuity within the park. A
refi ned theme can add a tremendous amount of interest and uniqueness to a place
that sparks the imagination of visitors without adding substantial cost. Architecture
site detailing, furniture, paving materials, layout and plantings will all evolve based
upon theming concepts.
Building on the concepts formed in the West Edge, there are great opportunities to
layer meaning and depth into the Civic Center Commons design. This may include
interpreting Cheyenne’s history or drawing from regional geology or ecosystems for
inspiration.  The combination of rich history and great forward thinking opportunity
provides a rich palette to work from.
