In an eff ort to improve our construction document quality control, we have developed
a plan checklist to ensure our construction documents are complete and well-
coordinated with other consultants. Contractors are bidding projects very low and
looking for opportunities for change orders. Documents must be concise to avoid
change orders that impact client’s budgets.
Core Park Components
At the core of the emerging West Edge neighborhood, Civic Commons Park will provide
an inviting outdoor setting. We believe that the core components of the park include
shade from the tree canopy, landscaping, views of the water, seating, spaces for a range
of activities, and artwork. Our approach is to structure the fi rst phase of the project in
anticipation of multiple future phases as funding becomes available. Grading, walls,
and surface treatments are all important considerations in the predesign phase to set
up the park to accommodate later additions.
Gathering spaces – providing open spaces on paved and lawn spaces sized to
accommodate a variety of smaller events and activities including inviting passive park use.
Landscaping – Shade trees, lawn and ornamental plantings are core elements
in both classical and contemporary successful parks because they create a
microenvironment off ering visual interest. Further, plantings that are designed
to enhance water quality fi ltering hold the potential to provide educational/
demonstration value along with ornamental qualities.
Trees – Canopy trees that contribute to the perimeter streetscape and add to the
formal street tree environment of downtown create an inviting, sheltered atmosphere.
Seating – The required grading to achieve the detention functions creates an opportunity
to provide seat height walls that double as ample seating for passive use within the park.
Stormwater Management
With no doubt that the stormwater system needs greater capacity, the strategy of
layering civic amenities for the infrastructure improvements matches well with the
objectives of the federal funding programs. These programs award grants implementing
the improvements, and is at the core of the West Edge vision.
Maintenance
Beyond understanding the objectives of the funding sources, our team believes that
a strong understanding of Cheyenne and the future required maintenance labor,
upkeep, security and lifecycle costs is critical to achieve success.
While stormwater parks are not new nationally, they have not been a major part of
the City's inventory.
