### Page 36 of 57

```text
29 | West Edge District Civic Center Commons
Our experience includes working closely with municipal public works and parks
departments for decades on customizing these types of parks to make them suitable
and manageable.
The following issues regarding maintenance would be addressed in our design process:
• Meet early with respective department heads to identify responsibilities between
departments.
• Match improvements to existing practices and equipment, and identify and agree
on any exceptions.
• Prepare a summary of areas and components, and associated labor and equipment
requirements.
• Set schedule of replacements and substantial regular maintenance activities.
• Project life span for proposed components, including water quality basins, sized
to be effi  cient but maximize life span.
• Design routes for vehicle access to all areas that need regular maintenance.
• Document key design standards and materials.
• Minimum and max slopes such as 3:1 max in landscape areas for mowing and
hand work.
• Minimum distance between trees to accommodate mower size.
• Standard city furnishings and irrigation system vs. custom.
• Retaining wall sizes and materials.
A primary challenge of combining a park and detention facility is grading the site to
handle fl ood volumes while maintaining an attractive and durable park space.

Water Conservation and Effi  cient Irrigation
Wyoming, being a semi-arid state, receives an average annual precipitation of only
15-16”. With Wyoming's water resource challenges, specifi c grass species (sod vs.
seed) considerations need to be reviewed from the start of every project. Irrigation
demands, regional climate, durability, maintenance and more are all factors that we
try to address early in the process. Working in collaboration with local horticulturists,
Catherine Wisner and irrigation consultant Tim Grote, former Cheyenne resident, will
help match appropriate water conscious planting while achieving overall project
objectives.
Wetlands Ecology
With ground water likely with excavation, our internal ecologists will assist with
selection of appropriate constructed wetland and riparian type plantings as appropriate
to Civic Center Commons that create a beautiful park setting in addition to meeting
maintenance and drainage system objectives. DHM Design brings specialized wetland
ecology expertise in-house to design drainage systems that are best suited to the site
conditions. Our ecologists provide critical input on soil profi les, infi ltration, channels,
and maintenance to ensure a successful installation.
Handling Core Challenges
 “Will the park be underwater when it rains?”
Working in close coordination, Benchmark Engineers and DHM Design will model
key volumes and water levels.  With extensive experience with detention ponds, we
generally expect to work around three primary levels:
Park Perimeter: The upper levels of the detention pond are very rarely inundated
and drain quickly after larger storm events.  This level is most suitable for providing
pedestrian oriented amenities.  Walls that help step down to the lower levels of the
park are an important design consideration regarding their height, materials, safety
and appearance. Around the park perimeter, there will be features that complement
the surrounding uses and contribute to the urban downtown streetscape. DHM Design
would help note features that are important in the basic framework of the park, versus
elements that could be phased in later.
Mid level:  Above the low fl ow, the mid-levels of the park would submerge temporarily
during larger fl ood events as part of the regional storm water system. These park
elements, walls and pavements would be designed to handle inundation similar to
river bank situations where spring runoff  surges annually. Grassy areas and basic
paving can be designed to withstand this type of occasional and temporary fl ooding.
```

