```text
DHM Design | 30
Low fl ow:  Within the storm water function of the park, directing smaller more frequent
storm events, combined with excavating into ground water zones creates a condition
of consistently high moisture level soils. This presents an opportunity to support
attractive riparian and wetland type plantings that not only fi lter runoff , but also
provides rich ornamental quality and texture to the park.
What components of the park may be new to Cheyenne and what are their maintenance
requirements?
The Treatment Train:
In similar parks that handle storm water volumes, our approach uses a linear system
of components designed to minimize maintenance.  We believe in designing around
using existing City equipment and practices, and proactively anticipating required
labor that be associated for the new improvements.
This generally includes:
1. A forebay, or structured setting basin for larger sediment.
2. Energy dissipation or pools that reduce velocities and limit scour.
3. Vegetated porous landscape detention basins if needed.
4. Detention and retention basins.
These elements are sized to optimize performance of the larger system and can be
designed to complement the park aesthetics and demonstrate their function in an
educational way.
Safety
The design of the park can help steer the locations that people are encouraged to use.
Areas for people can be concentrated around the upper perimeter.
Another key aspect is designing the slopes of the pond to preserve visibility. It is best
to use slopes that either step down, or include guard railings above.
An advantage of a sloped/stepped approach is that it works well with providing an
amphitheater setting that can host events and create passive seating areas.
We typically meet with the sheriff ’s offi  ce and emergency response to ensure proper access
and address potential problems areas that can be set up for success within the design.
Economic Activity
The Visionary Blueprint describes how additional public input will be included during
the design process for these public facilities.  We know how important it is to get the
details right for this project because it has the potential to launch millions of dollars of
investment if done correctly. The public process is not just a chance to gain required
public input but also a great opportunity to promote the value of this new amenity to
the surroundings and attract more development interest. The Park gains importance
as one of many investments that together build the critical mass. The Dinneen’s
championed this area for decades. The Park is very important to investors such as the
new steamplant owners and other local business owners advancing development
eff orts based on the West Edge vision.
By coordinating with the City events and promotional staff , we will integrate spaces
around the perimeter that can host a range of activities and events that draw people
downtown and extend their stays. By continuing the dialog we have begun with
adjacent property owners and the City, a balance of beautifi cation and functionality
can be achieved.  Our goal is to shape a park that posts a return on investment in
on-going economic activity, and provides long term value to the City.
